<article class="env-lemma" id="07IJ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07IJ">Lemma <span data-tag="07IJ">60.9.2</span>.</a> Assumptions as in Definition <a href="/tag/07I6" data-tag="07I6">60.8.1</a>. The inclusion functor </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Cris}(X/S) \to \text{CRIS}(X/S)  \]
</div>
  <p> commutes with finite nonempty limits, is fully faithful, continuous, and cocontinuous. There are morphisms of topoi </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  (X/S)_{\text{cris}} \xrightarrow {i} (X/S)_{\text{CRIS}} \xrightarrow {\pi } (X/S)_{\text{cris}}  \]
</div>
  <p> whose composition is the identity and of which the first is induced by the inclusion functor. Moreover, $\pi _* = i^{-1}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      For the first assertion see Lemma <a href="/tag/07I9" data-tag="07I9">60.8.2</a>. This gives us a morphism of topoi $i : (X/S)_{\text{cris}} \to (X/S)_{\text{CRIS}}$ and a left adjoint $i_!$ such that $i^{-1}i_! = i^{-1}i_* = \text{id}$, see Sites, Lemmas <a href="/tag/00XR" data-tag="00XR">7.21.5</a>, <a href="/tag/00XS" data-tag="00XS">7.21.6</a>, and <a href="/tag/00XT" data-tag="00XT">7.21.7</a>. We claim that $i_!$ is exact. If this is true, then we can define $\pi $ by the rules $\pi ^{-1} = i_!$ and $\pi _* = i^{-1}$ and everything is clear. To prove the claim, note that we already know that $i_!$ is right exact and preserves fibre products (see references given). Hence it suffices to show that $i_! * = *$ where $*$ indicates the final object in the category of sheaves of sets. To see this it suffices to produce a set of objects $(U_ i, T_ i, \delta _ i)$, $i \in I$ of $\text{Cris}(X/S)$ such that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \coprod \nolimits _{i \in I} h_{(U_ i, T_ i, \delta _ i)} \to *  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is surjective in $(X/S)_{\text{CRIS}}$ (details omitted; hint: use that $\text{Cris}(X/S)$ has products and that the functor $\text{Cris}(X/S) \to \text{CRIS}(X/S)$ commutes with them). In the affine case this follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/07HP" data-tag="07HP">60.5.6</a>. We omit the proof in general. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>